# space_station.k26asm - the passive vehicle of the docking benchmark.
#
# WHAT IS CITED. Mass and overall dimensions are the International
# Space Station's, as NASA publishes them on its space station facts
# and figures page, read on 2026-08-15 and carrying a page date of
# 16 July 2024:
#
#     mass                       925 335 pounds (419 725 kilograms)
#     truss length               310 feet (94 metres)
#     pressurised module length  218 feet along the major axis
#                                (67 metres)
#     solar array length         239 feet across both longitudinally
#                                aligned arrays (73 metres)
#
# WHAT IS MODELLED. NASA publishes lengths, not a bounding box, so
# assigning the three to the three body axes is this asset's choice
# and not a measurement: the pressurised modules run along the docking
# axis, the truss across it, and the arrays on the third axis. The
# collider is one box of those half extents, and the derivation treats
# it as a solid of uniform density, which is the only mass
# distribution this asset can honestly claim to know.
#
# WHAT THAT COSTS, STATED. A uniform solid of the station's overall
# extent puts mass further from the centre than the station does, so
# the derived inertia OVERSTATES. Against the 350-tonne design-case
# article of the docking standard (IDSS IDD Revision E, October 2016,
# Table 3.3.1.2-1: 3.50e5 kg, Ixx 1.15e8, Iyy 6.20e7, Izz 1.65e8
# kg m^2) the derived tensor is 4.3, 5.5 and 2.8 times its entries,
# on a mass 1.20 times the article's. The article is a class
# representative and not a model of any particular station, so
# agreement to a factor is the most either can assert about the other;
# the factors are recorded here so that a reader can see which way the
# figure errs rather than being told it is right.
#
# The docking interface is placed 0.1 m ahead of the box's forward
# face so that it is the first geometry an arriving craft meets. Its
# distance from the centre of mass, 33.6 m, is this asset's own
# geometry; the standard's 350-tonne article puts its hard capture
# mating plane at 20.0 m, which is the class figure and not this
# station's.

assembly space_station
    frame x_to_port

    provenance mass "NASA, International Space Station Facts and Figures, page dated 16 July 2024: 925 335 pounds (419 725 kilograms)" cited
    provenance dimensions "NASA, same page: truss 310 ft (94 m), pressurised module length 218 ft along the major axis (67 m), solar array length 239 ft across both arrays (73 m)" cited
    provenance hull_geometry "modelled: one box of the cited half extents, the three published lengths assigned to the docking axis, the truss axis and the third axis; NASA publishes lengths and not a bounding box" computed
    provenance inertia "computed from the cited mass and the modelled box at uniform density; it overstates, being 4.3, 5.5 and 2.8 times the 350-tonne design-case article of IDSS IDD Rev E Table 3.3.1.2-1" computed
    provenance port_dock "modelled: the mating plane 0.1 m ahead of the box's forward face so it is the first geometry an arriving craft meets" computed

    component structure
        mass 419725.0
        at 0.0 0.0 0.0
        collider box 33.5 47.0 36.5
    end

    port dock
        at 33.6 0.0 0.0
        axis 1.0 0.0 0.0
        roll_ref 0.0 1.0 0.0
        capture idss_e
    end
end
